.TH LSEEK 2
.SH NAME
lseek, tell \- move read/write pointer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B long lseek(int fildes, long offset, int whence);
.PP
.B long tell(int fildes);
.SH DESCRIPTION
The file
descriptor refers to a file open for reading or writing.
The read (resp. write) pointer for the file is set as follows:
.IP
If
.I whence
is 0, the pointer is set to
.I offset
bytes.
.IP
If
.I whence
is 1, the pointer is set to its current location plus
.IR offset .
.IP
If
.I whence
is 2, the pointer is set to the size of the
file plus
.IR offset .
.PP
The returned value is the resulting pointer location.
.PP
The obsolete function
.IR tell ( fildes )
is identical to
.IR lseek ( "fildes, 0L, 1" ).
.PP
Seeking far beyond the end of a file, then writing,
creates a gap or `hole', which occupies no
physical space and reads as zeros.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
open(2), creat(2), fseek(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
\-1
is returned for
an undefined file descriptor,
seek on a pipe,
or seek to a position before the beginning of file.
.SH BUGS
.I Lseek
is a no-op on character special files.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.B An offset is one word, and the gate lost an argument because of it.
.PP
.B off_t
is
.B int
is
.BR long ,
all one 48-bit word, so v7's two-word offset collapses to one.  The kernel's
.I sysent
entry said the call takes four arguments \- a PDP-11 leftover where the offset was
a two-word
.B long
\- and it says three now, matching the argument struct the kernel's own
.IR seek ()
reads.
.PP
.B That count is the contract nothing else can express.
A stub carries no arity; the kernel reads it from
.I sysent
and b6sim from its own table, and every caller's prototype must agree with both.
A count that disagrees reads the arguments from the wrong slots
.I and
drifts the caller's stack by a word per call.  See
.IR intro (2).
.PP
.I Tell
is declared by no header \- v7 had none \- so a caller declares it itself.
.IR Stime (2)
shed an argument in exactly the same way, its
.B time_t
having been two PDP-11 words.
.SH ASSEMBLER
.B Note:
v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here \- a decimal call number and a
`\fBsys\fP \fIname\fP; \fIarg\fP ...' line.  Neither is how a call is made on this
machine.  The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_seek
\- and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n\-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.
.PP
.B The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.
The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is the only place a syscall number
appears on the user side.  See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
